msgctxt "#32091"
msgid "Recreate local NFO file from Chronicle's data"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#32092"
msgid "Kodi always prefers a TV show or episode's own local NFO file over asking this scraper, even during an automatic library scan -- so anything already tagged by another tool (e.g. tinyMediaManager) may never actually reach Chronicle at all. Enabling this lets Chronicle write that local NFO from its own data -- but only as part of an explicit rebuild pass (\"Rebuild local NFOs from Chronicle\" or \"Automatically rebuild NFOs after every library scan\", both in Chronicle Scraper (Movies)'s own settings, covering movies and TV shows together), never during an ordinary scan, so getting new files into Kodi's library is never slowed down by a local-file write. Off by default -- if you have more than one Kodi instance pointed at the same shared library, you only need this ON for whichever one is responsible for maintaining the shared NFOs (e.g. your main box); the others just read the NFO that instance already wrote."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#32105"
msgid "Include technical file info (resolution, codec, HDR, audio, subtitles)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "#32106"
msgid "Adds a <fileinfo><streamdetails> block to the NFO with Kodi's own probe of the real file -- video codec/resolution/HDR type, audio codec/language/channels, subtitle track languages. This is data only Kodi itself has; Chronicle has no way to supply it. Requires an extra lookup against Kodi's own library on top of the normal NFO write, so it's off by default -- if you run this addon on more than one Kodi instance pointed at the same shared library, you only need this ON for whichever one is responsible for maintaining the shared NFOs; the others just read the NFO that instance already wrote, streamdetails included, so there's nothing to gain (and real extra work to lose) by also turning this on for them."
msgstr ""

